Now I see what's happening! The second call to `__getattr__` with `attr='prop'` is coming directly from the module level, not from inside the property getter. This suggests that Python is trying to access the `prop` attribute again after the property getter failed.

This is likely because of how Python's attribute access mechanism works with descriptors and `__getattr__`. When a property raises an AttributeError, Python might be falling back to `__getattr__` to see if the attribute can be found there.

Let me check if this is the case by looking at what happens when we don't override `__getattr__`:{"arguments": "{\"command\": \"create\", \"path\": \"/testbed/test_no_getattr.py\", \"file_text\": \"import astropy.coordinates as coord\\n\\n\\nclass custom_coord_no_getattr(coord.SkyCoord):\\n    @property\\n    def prop(self):\\n        print(\\\"Inside prop getter\\\")\\n        return self.random_attr\\n    \\n    # No __getattr__ override\\n\\n\\nc = custom_coord_no_getattr('00h42m30s', '+41d12m00s', frame='icrs')\\ntry:\\n    c.prop\\nexcept AttributeError as e:\\n    print(f\\\"Error message: {e}\\\")\"}", "name": "str_replace_editor"}

Wait, this approach still doesn't give us the original error message. Let me think of a better approach. 

The real issue is that we want to preserve the original AttributeError that was raised inside the property, not create a new generic one. 

Let me try a different approach. Instead of trying to detect when a descriptor raised an AttributeError, let's modify the approach to try to get the original error. We can do this by attempting to access the attribute again and catching the specific error:{"arguments": "{\"command\": \"str_replace\", \"path\": \"/testbed/astropy/coordinates/sky_coordinate.py\", \"old_str\": \"        # Check if the attribute is a descriptor (property, method, etc.) in the class hierarchy.\\n        # If it is, it means the descriptor exists but raised AttributeError internally.\\n        # In this case, we should not handle it here but let the original error propagate.\\n        for cls in type(self).__mro__:\\n            if attr in cls.__dict__:\\n                class_attr = cls.__dict__[attr]\\n                # Check if it's a descriptor (has __get__, __set__, or __delete__)\\n                if hasattr(class_attr, '__get__') or hasattr(class_attr, '__set__') or hasattr(class_attr, '__delete__'):\\n                    # This is a descriptor that exists but raised AttributeError.\\n                    # We should not claim the attribute doesn't exist.\\n                    # Instead, re-raise AttributeError without our custom message.\\n                    raise AttributeError(f\\\"'{self.__class__.__name__}' object attribute '{attr}' raised an AttributeError\\\")\", \"new_str\": \"        # Check if the attribute is a descriptor (property, method, etc.) in the class hierarchy.\\n        # If it is, it means the descriptor exists but raised AttributeError internally.\\n        # In this case, we should not handle it here but let the original error propagate.\\n        for cls in type(self).__mro__:\\n            if attr in cls.__dict__:\\n                class_attr = cls.__dict__[attr]\\n                # Check if it's a descriptor (has __get__, __set__, or __delete__)\\n                if hasattr(class_attr, '__get__') or hasattr(class_attr, '__set__') or hasattr(class_attr, '__delete__'):\\n                    # This is a descriptor that exists but raised AttributeError.\\n                    # Try to call it again to get the original error message.\\n                    try:\\n                        class_attr.__get__(self, type(self))\\n                    except AttributeError as original_error:\\n                        # Re-raise the original error\\n                        raise original_error\\n                    except Exception:\\n                        # If it raises a different exception, fall through to normal handling\\n                        pass\"}", "name": "str_replace_editor"}

