Let me check if there's any shared state between the original and derived querysets:{"arguments": "{\"command\": \"str_replace\", \"path\": \"/testbed/test_specific_issue.py\", \"old_str\": \"print(\\\"\\\\nTrying to re-evaluate original queryset...\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Original queryset SQL after derived: {qs.query}\\\")\\n# This is where the bug might occur - the original queryset should still work\\n# but it might break if the ordering references a column position that no longer exists\\ntry:\\n    # Let's try to get the compiler and see what happens\\n    from django.db.models.sql.compiler import SQLCompiler\\n    compiler = qs.query.get_compiler(using='default')\\n    print(f\\\"Compiler select: {compiler.select}\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"Trying to get order_by...\\\")\\n    order_by = compiler.get_order_by()\\n    print(f\\\"Order by: {order_by}\\\")\\n    \\n    original_result = list(qs)\\n    print(f\\\"Original queryset result: {[dim.id for dim in original_result]}\\\")\\n    print(\\\"SUCCESS: Original queryset re-evaluated without error\\\")\\nexcept Exception as e:\\n    print(f\\\"ERROR: {e}\\\")\\n    import traceback\\n    traceback.print_exc()\", \"new_str\": \"print(\\\"\\\\nTrying to re-evaluate original queryset...\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Original queryset SQL after derived: {qs.query}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Original queryset id: {id(qs.query)}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Derived queryset id: {id(derived_qs.query)}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Are they the same object? {qs.query is derived_qs.query}\\\")\\n\\n# Check if the combined_queries are shared\\nprint(f\\\"Original combined_queries id: {id(qs.query.combined_queries)}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Derived combined_queries id: {id(derived_qs.query.combined_queries)}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Are combined_queries the same? {qs.query.combined_queries is derived_qs.query.combined_queries}\\\")\\n\\nif qs.query.combined_queries:\\n    print(f\\\"Original first combined query id: {id(qs.query.combined_queries[0])}\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"Derived first combined query id: {id(derived_qs.query.combined_queries[0])}\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"Are first combined queries the same? {qs.query.combined_queries[0] is derived_qs.query.combined_queries[0]}\\\")\\n\\n# This is where the bug might occur - the original queryset should still work\\n# but it might break if the ordering references a column position that no longer exists\\ntry:\\n    original_result = list(qs)\\n    print(f\\\"Original queryset result: {[dim.id for dim in original_result]}\\\")\\n    print(\\\"SUCCESS: Original queryset re-evaluated without error\\\")\\nexcept Exception as e:\\n    print(f\\\"ERROR: {e}\\\")\\n    import traceback\\n    traceback.print_exc()\"}", "name": "str_replace_editor"}

The issue is at line 934! The `combined_queries` is set to a tuple containing the original query objects (`self.query` and `qs.query` for each queryset). These are not cloned, so they are shared between the original and derived querysets.

When the derived queryset modifies these shared query objects (e.g., by calling `set_values`), it affects the original queryset as well.

Let me look at how the query cloning works to understand the fix:{"arguments": "{\"command\": \"view\", \"path\": \"/testbed/django/db/models/sql/query.py\", \"view_range\": [292, 340]}", "name": "str_replace_editor"}

The issue is that when we clone a query, the `combined_queries` is copied by reference (shallow copy) at line 300 in the clone method: `obj.__dict__ = self.__dict__.copy()`. This means that the `combined_queries` tuple is shared, but more importantly, the individual query objects within that tuple are also shared.

The fix is to ensure that when we clone a query that has `combined_queries`, we also clone the individual queries within `combined_queries`.

Let me implement the fix:{"arguments": "{\"command\": \"str_replace\", \"path\": \"/testbed/django/db/models/sql/query.py\", \"old_str\": \"        obj.used_aliases = self.used_aliases.copy()\\n        obj._filtered_relations = self._filtered_relations.copy()\\n        # Clear the cached_property\\n        try:\\n            del obj.base_table\\n        except AttributeError:\\n            pass\\n        return obj\", \"new_str\": \"        obj.used_aliases = self.used_aliases.copy()\\n        obj._filtered_relations = self._filtered_relations.copy()\\n        # Clone combined_queries to avoid sharing query objects\\n        if self.combined_queries:\\n            obj.combined_queries = tuple(query.clone() for query in self.combined_queries)\\n        # Clear the cached_property\\n        try:\\n            del obj.base_table\\n        except AttributeError:\\n            pass\\n        return obj\"}", "name": "str_replace_editor"}

